Build libgcc and libstdc++ with POSIX threads support

When GCC is cross-compiled to run on LakerLinux (the devtools stage), its
build would otherwise pick the "single-threaded" thread header for libgcc and
libstdc++, and C++ programs couldn't use std::thread. This forces the POSIX
one. Linux From Scratch makes the same change (chapter 6, "GCC-15.2.0 -
Pass 2").

diff --git a/libgcc/Makefile.in b/libgcc/Makefile.in
index 0719fd0..6241384 100644
--- a/libgcc/Makefile.in
+++ b/libgcc/Makefile.in
@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@ unwind_header = @unwind_header@
 md_unwind_def_header = @md_unwind_def_header@
 md_unwind_header = @md_unwind_header@
 sfp_machine_header = @sfp_machine_header@
-thread_header = @thread_header@
+thread_header = gthr-posix.h
 
 host_noncanonical = @host_noncanonical@
 real_host_noncanonical = @real_host_noncanonical@
diff --git a/libstdc++-v3/include/Makefile.in b/libstdc++-v3/include/Makefile.in
index 7b96b22..f072513 100644
--- a/libstdc++-v3/include/Makefile.in
+++ b/libstdc++-v3/include/Makefile.in
@@ -345,7 +345,7 @@ target_alias = @target_alias@
 target_cpu = @target_cpu@
 target_os = @target_os@
 target_vendor = @target_vendor@
-thread_header = @thread_header@
+thread_header = gthr-posix.h
 tmake_file = @tmake_file@
 top_build_prefix = @top_build_prefix@
 top_builddir = @top_builddir@
